# DTF tier manifest: maven-grouped-name-keyset  (#2723)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single.
#
# Sibling of `maven-grouped-name` (which gates the catalog WRITE path:
# packages.name == groupId:artifactId). This tier gates the second half of
# #2723 -- the LISTING path. Hosted/virtual Maven grouped listings
# (`GET .../artifacts?group_by=maven_component`) are now served through the
# package-catalog SQL keyset (bounded memory), replacing the prior
# fetch-everything-then-group-in-memory path that never advertised a cursor.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh) is the discriminating gate. After uploading two Maven
# artifacts under distinct groupId:artifactId to a hosted maven repo, it walks
# the grouped listing one component per page via the opaque `?cursor=`:
#   * Fixed (#2723): page 1 carries a non-null `next_cursor`/`has_more=true`,
#     and following the cursor returns the SECOND, distinct component keyed on
#     `groupId:artifactId`, in stable ascending order.
#   * Pre-#2723 listing: the in-memory grouped path emits NO `next_cursor`
#     (skip_serializing_if None) and ignores `?cursor=`, so the cursor page
#     repeats the first component -> FAIL. (This holds even though the catalog
#     `packages.name` is already grouped by the merged write-path fix, so the
#     gate isolates the listing/keyset change specifically.)
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + two uploads + two
# listing reads back-to-back; the base default (ON) could throttle them.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
